  "id": "hu-kezdo-betupar",
  "description": "Permitted opening letter pairs of the Hungarian registration mark introduced on 2022-07-01. § 53 (6) of 326/2011. (XII. 28.) Korm. rendelet prescribes that the plate starts with \"két magánhangzóból vagy két mássalhangzóból - kivéve a cs, gy, ly, ny, sz, ty, zs betűkombinációkat\": two vowels or two consonants of the Latin alphabet, minus the seven Hungarian digraphs. This table is that rule enumerated, not a separate authority list: 25 vowel pairs over A, E, I, O, U (accented vowels are barred by the same paragraph) plus 441 consonant pairs over the remaining 21 letters minus CS, GY, LY, NY, SZ, TY and ZS = 434, 459 pairs, of which 458 are listed here. Y is treated as a consonant, which is what makes the explicit GY, LY, NY and TY exclusions meaningful — a vowel Y would already have excluded them as mixed pairs. Mixed vowel/consonant pairs (AB, BA) are absent because the paragraph admits neither. The pair TX is withheld: annex 13/A point 3 gives the TX-lettered taxi plate exactly the remaining shape of an ordinary mark (two letters after the coat of arms, a hyphen and three digits), so leaving TX in the serial pairs would make every Hungarian taxi plate AMBIGUOUS. § 53 (6) does not itself exclude it — this is the reservation in annex 13/A read as narrowing the serial series, and it is the only such narrowing applied: CD stays in the list because the diplomatic mark of annex 13/A point 1 is six digits and cannot be confused with a serial one.",
